---
id: com.example.timeline
name: Timeline
version: 0.1.0
---

A chart of a sheet range with one event per row (date, category, title, description, image), drawn along a vertical
spine, kept as its own object so documents can embed it as a section or a slide.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.
Each extension is defined in the Appendix or resolved via its URI.

| Term       | URI                         |
| ---------- | --------------------------- |
| `type`     | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`     |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`     |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0` |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0` |

## Types

```mdl
type Chart
  desc: A chart of a range of a sheet, owned by the sheet.
  fields:
    sheet: Ref<Sheet>          # the sheet whose range is drawn
    name?: string
    range: string              # A1 notation (e.g. "A1:E40")
    type: string               # bar | pie | xy | scatter | heatmap | radar | map | timeline
    header?: boolean           # the first row holds the column names
    timeline?: TimelineSettings
```

```mdl
type TimelineSettings
  desc: How a timeline chart is drawn.
  fields:
    spine?: string             # left | center | right
    scale?: string             # log | linear | compact; compact by default
    start?: string             # year or date
    end?: string               # year or date
    dates?: boolean            # show each event's date beside its title
    future?: boolean           # show events after today; off by default
    hidden?: string[]          # categories not drawn
    order?: string[]           # categories in layout order
    colors?: Map<string, string>
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Chart objects
  desc: Each chart is its own object, so a document can embed it.

  req F-1.1: A sheet holds its charts as refs; each `type:Chart` refs its sheet back and is deleted with it.
  req F-1.2: Sheets stored with inline charts are migrated to chart objects when their space opens.
  req F-1.3: A chart renders as an article, a section in a document, and a slide.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Timeline chart
  desc: Events from a sheet range along a vertical spine.

  req F-2.1: Each row is one event: date, category, title, and an optional description and image URL.
  req F-2.2: The spine sits left, center or right; spacing is log, linear or compact, animated when it changes.
  req F-2.3: Categories nest by path (`Tech/AI`) and toggle from tags; ⌘-click shows only one.
  req F-2.4: Clicking or the arrow keys select an event and show its card; Escape unselects.
  req F-2.5: A text filter narrows the events and reports how many match.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Slide embeds
  desc: A presented markdown deck shows an embedded object full-viewport.

  req F-3.1: `![label](echo:…)` in a deck renders the object's slide surface over the whole viewport.
  req F-3.2: While an embed shows, the deck acts on no key but Escape.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Presenter space
  desc: A space template with an events sheet, its timeline chart, and a deck built from a markdown template.

  req F-4.1: The deck's `{{timeline}}` placeholder is replaced with the created chart's EID.
```

F-5, the timeline research skill, is named on the project's feature map and is not written yet: it researches the
events for a range of years and a set of categories and fills a sheet with them.

## Phases

- Phase 1: F-1 and F-2, checked against the test fixture (the Presenter space's events sheet).
- Phase 2: F-3 and F-4.
- Phase 3: F-5.

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: A timeline chart of the fixture
  given: the events sheet and a timeline chart of its range
  when: the chart opens
  then:
    - each row is drawn as an event in its category's lane
    - future events are hidden until shown
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: A chart in a slide
  given: a deck whose second slide embeds the timeline chart
  when: the deck is presented and moves to the second slide
  then:
    - the chart fills the viewport without its toolbars
    - the arrow keys move the chart's selection, not the deck
```

